<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pan-Arabism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pan-Arabism is the ideology that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> form a single nation and should be united politically. It grew from the Arab cultural revival and anti-Ottoman nationalism of the late 19th and early 20th centuries and was championed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'ath Party</a> and above all by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a>. Its high point was the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> as the United Arab Republic (1958–1961), and it declined after the Arab defeat of 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pan-arabism.jpg" alt="Pan-Arabism" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Faycal.09 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arab_world_location_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
